what: (1) establish what actually ends the runner process, by reading
  the runner's own sources (test-runner 0.5.12: ProcessRunner,
  ComRunner, NativeRunnerRPC, TestMain, NativeRPC) rather than the
  symptom; (2) MEASURE the exit-code map by driving a real okay Native
  test binary from a harness that ends the connection four ways;
  (3) rule the remaining suspects in or out BY COMMAND; (4) rewrite
  the entry with what is proven, what is refuted and what is still
  open; (5) land the harness so the map can be re-established rather
  than re-argued.

found so far (all before this claim was written):
  - the log's exception carries NO cause and there is no "Force close",
    no "finished with non-zero value", no "fatal signal" — and those
    three are exactly the lines the runner prints on every non-zero
    exit. So the process exited 0, cleanly.
  - measured on okay-lex-test: JVM closes the socket -> exit 0 silent;
    a zero-length message -> exit 0 silent; SIGTERM -> 143; SIGKILL ->
    137. The two signal shapes are the ones that WOULD have been
    logged, and were not.
  - ruled out by command: RAM guard (killed=0, only SPARE lines in the
    window), jetsam/OS kill (empty memorystatus window, no crash
    report), our own code (no exit call in any Native-reachable
    source), the plugin's global adapter close (30+ runner processes
    started FINE afterwards, and its warn is absent), adapter GC
    (registerResource holds strong references).

predict: the open link — what ended that one connection — will NOT be
  settled by this claim. It is narrowed to two silent-by-construction
  candidates and the entry will say what evidence separates them.
  Recorded so that a claim that ends in a narrowing rather than a fix
  is visible as such.
